## 🔴 Solution 2: Block-Based Partitioning (BlockQuicksort Style)

**Problem**: Element-by-element partitioning causes frequent branches.

**Solution**: Process elements in blocks of 64, compare all at once:

```cpp
constexpr size_t BLOCK_SIZE = 64;

template<typename T, typename Compare>
void partition_block(T* a, std::ptrdiff_t low, std::ptrdiff_t high, 
                     T pivot1, T pivot2, Compare comp,
                     std::ptrdiff_t& lt, std::ptrdiff_t& gt) 
{
    // Buffer for elements that need to move left/right
    alignas(64) T left_buffer[BLOCK_SIZE];
    alignas(64) T right_buffer[BLOCK_SIZE];
    alignas(64) uint8_t left_indices[BLOCK_SIZE];
    alignas(64) uint8_t right_indices[BLOCK_SIZE];

    size_t left_count = 0;
    size_t right_count = 0;

    // Scan a block of 64 elements, classify each
    for (size_t i = 0; i < BLOCK_SIZE && (low + i) <= gt; i++) {
        T val = a[low + i];
        bool goes_left = comp(val, pivot1);
        bool goes_right = comp(pivot2, val);

        // Store indices without branching
        left_indices[left_count] = i;
        left_count += goes_left;

        right_indices[right_count] = i;
        right_count += goes_right;
    }

    // Now do the actual swaps in bulk
    // This reduces branch misprediction to once per 64 elements
    for (size_t i = 0; i < left_count; i++) {
        std::swap(a[low + left_indices[i]], a[lt++]);
    }
    // ... similar for right
}
```

**Expected Improvement**: 20-30% reduction in branch mispredictions by amortizing branches over 64 elements.

