## Solution 5: False Sharing - What "Cache Line Ping-Pong" Means

### How CPU caches work

```
Cache Line = 64 bytes (fixed unit of transfer between cache levels)

When Core 0 reads address 0x1000:
→ CPU loads 64 bytes: 0x1000-0x103F into Core 0's L1 cache
→ Core 0 marks this cache line as "Exclusive" (only I have it)
```

### The problem: False Sharing

```cpp
// Your current threadpool.hpp
struct WorkStealingQueue {
    std::deque<...> q;  // ~32 bytes
    std::mutex mtx;     // ~40 bytes
    // Total: ~72 bytes
};

// In memory, consecutive queues might look like:
// Address:  0x1000                    0x1040                    0x1080
//           ├──────── 64 bytes ───────┼──────── 64 bytes ───────┤
//           │ Queue[0].q | Queue[0].mtx | Queue[1].q (partial) │
//           │ Thread 0 writes here      | Thread 1 writes here! │
//           └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//                      ↑ SAME CACHE LINE ↑
```

**What happens:**
1. Thread 0 modifies `Queue[0].mtx` (byte 48)
2. Thread 1 modifies `Queue[1].q` (byte 72)
3. **Both bytes are in the same 64-byte cache line!**
4. Cache coherency protocol kicks in:
   ```
   Core 0: "I modified this line" → broadcasts INVALIDATE
   Core 1: Must reload line from L3
   Core 1: "I modified this line" → broadcasts INVALIDATE
   Core 0: Must reload line from L3
   ... ping-pong forever ...
   ```

### The fix: Force each queue to its own cache line

```cpp
struct alignas(64) WorkStealingQueue {  // alignas(64) = start on 64-byte boundary
    std::deque<...> q;
    std::mutex mtx;
    // Compiler adds padding to make total size multiple of 64 bytes
};

// Now in memory:
// Address:  0x1000                    0x1040                    0x1080
//           ├──────── 64 bytes ───────┼──────── 64 bytes ───────┼
//           │ Queue[0] (padded)        │ Queue[1] (padded)        │
//           │ Thread 0's exclusive     │ Thread 1's exclusive     │
//           └──────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘
//               ↑ SEPARATE CACHE LINES ↑
```

